1 Welcome to Emacsvox
*********************

Welcome to Emacsvox, a keyboard-driven spoken environment built on
Emacs.  It helps you read and write text, browse the Web, use mail and
messaging, work in shells, program, and explore structured information
through speech and sound.

   Emacsvox requires Emacs 31 or newer and is derived from Emacspeak.
Emacspeak's pioneering audio-desktop design and T.  V.  Raman's original
work remain the foundation of this project.

   Emacsvox works inside Emacs rather than reading the screen as a grid
of pixels.  Because it can use commands, modes, text structure, and
application state, it can announce what an action means and what
changed.  Ordinary movement speaks the text crossed; different voices
convey structure; auditory icons and tones mark events without
interrupting you with unnecessary words.

1.1 Where To Begin
==================

   • If Emacsvox is not installed yet, start with *note Installation::.
     It takes you from a source checkout to a clean speaking session and
     includes a recovery path if speech does not start.

   • If Emacsvox is already speaking, continue with *note Basic Usage::
     for a short first-session guide and the essential reading commands.

   • If Emacs itself is new to you, ‘C-h t’ opens the Emacs tutorial.
     Emacsvox speaks the tutorial and the commands you use while
     following it.

   • In any Emacs buffer, ‘C-h m’ describes the active modes and their
     commands, while ‘C-h k’ followed by a key explains that key.  See
     *note Using Online Help:: when you want to explore further.

1.2 What You Will Hear
======================

Emacsvox combines several kinds of feedback:

   • speech for text, prompts, results, and status;
   • voice changes that make syntax and document structure audible;
   • short auditory icons and tones for events such as opening a file or
     completing an action; and
   • pauses and spatial cues that help separate related information.

   You can adjust this presentation gradually.  Aural Home, opened with
‘C-e H’, brings voices, sound packs, profiles, and other presentation
options together.  You do not need to configure it before beginning.
See *note Aural Presentation:: when you want to understand or change
what you hear.

   Emacsvox sends speech through a speech server.  New GNU/Linux, macOS,
and WSL2 installations use Omnivox; established direct servers remain
available for compatible existing setups.  The installation chapter
introduces only what you need for first speech.  *Note Speech Backends::
contains the platform and compatibility details.

1.3 About This Manual
=====================

The maintained chapters provide the current Emacsvox setup and task
guides.  The separate generated reference records commands, options, and
keymaps from the source, while the heritage manual preserves Emacspeak
essays and application guides for their historical and design value.
Historical material may describe software or Web services from its
original period.

   Rapidly changing features are also documented in ‘README.org’,
‘etc/NEWS’, and the generated Aural Presentation reference.  When a
copied key binding differs from your running Emacs, prefer the live help
shown by ‘C-h m’ or ‘C-h k’.


