2.1 Before You Begin
====================

Install ‘git’ so you can obtain the Emacsvox checkout.  Emacsvox
requires Emacs 31 or newer
(https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html), but WSL2 users do
not need to find a separate Emacs package before beginning.  The guided
installer below uses an existing supported Emacs or builds the pinned
stable release for the current user.

   On GNU/Linux and macOS, install Emacs 31 or newer and ‘make’.
Omnivox 1.7.0 or newer is the supported speech-server baseline.
Installing its release archive also requires ‘curl’ and either
‘sha256sum’ on GNU/Linux or ‘shasum’ on macOS, plus ‘tar’.  GNU Texinfo
is needed only when building or editing the documentation, not for an
ordinary installation.

   To check the Emacs found by your shell, run:

     emacs --version

   The first line should begin with ‘GNU Emacs 31’ or a higher version
number.  If the command is not found or reports an older version,
install a supported Emacs or locate its executable.  You can check a
specific executable in the same way:

     /path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs --version

   The Linux x86-64 archive requires an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible glibc,
libstdc++, libgcc, and ALSA runtime.  If the archive does not match the
host, the Omnivox source route requires Rust, Python 3, a C and C++
toolchain, CMake, and Clang with libclang.  It additionally requires
‘pkg-config’ and ALSA development headers on GNU/Linux or the Xcode
command-line tools on macOS.  These are not requirements for the
recommended WSL2 binary route.  Docker and Windows .NET Framework are
needed only for the advanced reproducible source-build route in *note
Building Omnivox For WSL2::.

   Run the clone from a WSL terminal and keep the checkout on the Linux
filesystem.  The following concrete location works for an ordinary
per-user installation:

     mkdir -p ~/src
     cd ~/src
     git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
     cd emacsvox

   The Emacsvox checkout is the installation directory.  It builds in
place and does not create a second tree to copy elsewhere.  Do not put
the ordinary WSL2 checkout below a Windows-mounted path such as
‘/mnt/c’; Windows filesystem semantics can interfere with Unix
executable permissions and build tools.  Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox
only when following the Omnivox source-build or reproducible WSL2 build
route.

   Under WSL2, first ask the guided installer to inspect the machine
without changing it:

     cd ~/src/emacsvox
     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check

   The report names the Emacs it will use, the matching Omnivox Windows
archive, and the per-user installation paths.  If Emacs build packages
are missing, it prints one ‘apt’ command for you to review and run.  The
installer never invokes ‘sudo’ itself.  After resolving any reported
prerequisite, run:

     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install

   It builds the pinned stable Emacs only when Emacs 31 or newer is
unavailable, downloads and verifies the matching Omnivox 1.7.0 Windows
archive, records its path without editing a shell startup file, compiles
Emacsvox, runs a diagnostic, and plays the final speech-server check.
The Emacs build can take a while on a fresh machine; later runs reuse it
and the verified downloads.  Routine compiler output is kept below
‘$XDG_CACHE_HOME/emacsvox/logs’, normally ‘~/.cache/emacsvox/logs’.  If
a build fails, the installer shows its final log lines and the complete
log path.  Continue only after hearing both the final tone and speech.
You can then skip to the last step in *note Quick Installation:: and
start Emacsvox.


