2.3 Install And Check Omnivox
=============================

If you used the guided WSL2 installer above, Omnivox is already
installed and checked.  Continue to *note Quick Installation::.

   On a matching GNU/Linux x86-64 or macOS host, follow *note Installing
An Omnivox Release Binary::.  That procedure downloads and verifies the
published 1.7.0 archive, keeps its runtime data beside the executable,
and adds its versioned installation directory to ‘PATH’.  Then run:

     omnivox --check

   Use *note Installing Omnivox From Source:: instead on Linux ARM64, an
incompatible Linux distribution, or a host whose security policy does
not permit the unsigned macOS archive.  No proprietary speech runtime is
needed for either GNU/Linux or macOS route.

   For a manual WSL2 installation, or to understand exactly what the
guided installer verifies, see *note Installing An Omnivox Windows
Release Under WSL2::.

   The check plays a tone followed by speech.  When you hear both, the
speech server is ready.  If either is missing, use *note Backend
Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership.
before continuing.  *Note Speech Backends:: contains the complete
platform matrix, build details, and compatibility alternatives.  Native
Windows Emacs support remains a preview.  Evaluators can follow *note
Trying Native Windows Emacsvox::; initial users should choose the WSL2
route.


