2.5 Confirming First Speech
===========================

You will know the isolated start succeeded when Omnivox acknowledges the
initial engine inventory, routing policy, and logical voices, and
Emacsvox speaks:

     Emacsvox is ready.

   An earlier introduction, overview key, version number, and startup
auditory icon may also be heard.  Those can change; the sentence above
is the stable first-speech checkpoint because it normally occurs after
initial routing is acknowledged and therefore uses the restored engine
policy.  If routing does not finish within five seconds, Emacsvox speaks
the checkpoint through the capability-ready fallback so a routing error
cannot suppress it indefinitely.

   You now have a working Emacsvox session.  Try these checks before
connecting it to your personal configuration:

  1. Type ‘M-x tts-speak-version RET’.  The live speech adapter should
     speak its version response.

  2. Type ‘C-h v tts-program RET’.  The displayed current value should
     name ‘omnivox’.

  3. Use ‘C-n’ and ‘C-p’ to move over lines and confirm ordinary spoken
     navigation.  Type ‘M-x emacsvox-describe-emacsvox RET’ for the
     Emacsvox overview.  *Note Basic Usage::, for the tested starter
     commands.

   If the initial speech is too fast, press ‘C-e d -’ and then repeat
‘-’ to slow the current buffer, or use ‘C-e d r’ to enter an exact rate
such as ‘40’.  For individually echoed characters, use ‘C-e d f’ and try
a multiplier below one, such as ‘0.7’.  Put ‘C-u’ before the exact-rate
or character-factor command to change the global default rather than
only the current buffer.

   When these checks pass, continue to *note Basic Usage:: for a short
guided first session, or use *note Persistent Startup:: to connect
Emacsvox to your normal Emacs configuration.


