2.6 Recovering From No Speech
=============================

If speech did not start, keep the isolated launcher as your known
starting point and find the first command below that did not work:

‘./bin/emacsvox --diagnose’ fails
     No audio or interactive Emacs has started.  Under WSL2, begin with
     ‘./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check’ and resolve the first item it
     reports.  Otherwise, follow the launcher selection: set ‘EMACS’,
     correct ‘EMACS=’ in ‘local.mk’, or install Emacs 31 or newer when
     the Emacs check fails.  When the speech server is not executable,
     finish installing Omnivox, put it on ‘PATH’, or set
     ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ to its absolute executable.  The guided WSL2
     installer records the verified executable in a per-user
     configuration file; rerunning it repairs a missing installation or
     selection without requiring a shell startup edit.
The diagnostic passes, but ‘--check’ errors or is inaudible
     The speech server needs attention before Emacs configuration is
     relevant.  Follow *note Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities:
     Backend Diagnostics And Ownership. until the check produces both
     its tone and speech.
The audible check passes, but "Emacsvox is ready" is absent
     Run ‘./bin/emacsvox --diagnose’ again from the same checkout and
     shell.  Remove an unintended ‘TTS_PROGRAM’ override if the report
     does not select ‘omnivox’.  Then request an Emacs startup backtrace
     with:

          ./bin/emacsvox -- --debug-init

     An error saying ‘Could not find speech server executable’ means the
     speech server visible during that start cannot be executed.
     Correct the reported speech-server path rather than adding more
     init-file configuration.  For an Omnivox process error, inspect the
     private log directory described below.
The ready message is heard, but speech later stops
     If Emacs reports that an Emacsvox speech server exited, inspect the
     Omnivox session log and type ‘M-x tts-restart RET’ after correcting
     the speech-server problem.  Logs are below
     ‘$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox’, defaulting to
     ‘~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox’.  Spoken text is omitted unless
     explicit text logging is enabled.
The isolated launcher works, but a customized Emacs start does not
     Remove or comment out the Emacsvox environment and ‘load-file’
     forms from the personal init file.  This is the rollback: it leaves
     the checkout and the known-good ‘./bin/emacsvox’ path intact.
     Re-add the forms in the order shown in *note Persistent Startup::;
     speech-server selection must happen before setup is loaded.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Persistent Startup,  Prev: Recovering From No Speech,  Up: Installation

2.7 Persistent Startup
======================

After *note Confirming First Speech:: succeeds, add these forms to your
Emacs init file, replacing the example path with the absolute path of
the checkout:

     (setenv "TTS_PROGRAM" "omnivox")
     (load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")

   Keep the forms in this order: Emacsvox selects and starts the speech
server while loading setup.  A shell alternative is to export the server
name before launching Emacs:

     export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox

   Keep the explicit ‘load-file’ form for a changing source checkout.
Its setup entry point can warn and prefer source when newer Lisp would
otherwise be hidden by stale local byte-code.  A customized startup
should speak the same ready message as the isolated launcher.  If it
does not, remove or comment out the two init forms and return to the
isolated launcher before trying another change.


