3.3 Emacsvox Overview
=====================

Emacsvox adds spoken feedback to Emacs editing and to applications that
run inside Emacs.  Movement speaks the corresponding character, word,
line, or larger unit.  Commands announce useful results and state
changes, while application integrations present structures such as
messages, search results, tables, source code, and Web pages.

   Changes in voice, short auditory icons, and tones make structure and
familiar events recognizable without inserting extra words.
Pronunciation dictionaries can also correct names and technical terms
globally or for one mode or file.  You do not need to configure these
systems before beginning; first notice what the active mode presents
automatically.

   For an everyday Voice Lock experiment, see *note Hear Structure With
Voice Lock: Voice-lock.  When you want to understand or change voices,
sounds, pronunciation, or other presentation choices, continue with
*note Aural Presentation::.  The generated reference remains the exact
inventory of their commands and options.


