3.4 Using Emacs Buffers
=======================

As you type, character echo can speak individual characters and word
echo can speak a word when you finish it.  Completion interfaces
announce candidates, the inserted completion, and the item currently
selected.  Prompts and error messages are spoken through the same Emacs
feedback that appears on screen.

   Most everyday feedback happens automatically.  Emacsvox also provides
three useful groups of explicit commands:

   • Reading commands speak a character, word, line, region, or larger
     portion of a buffer without requiring you to move first.  Their
     names usually begin with ‘emacsvox-speak-’, and their default keys
     use the main ‘C-e’ prefix.(1)

   • Speech-system commands change settings such as rate, punctuation,
     and echo.  Their names usually begin with ‘tts-’, and many are
     grouped under ‘C-e d’.(2)

   • Help commands describe the active modes, available keys, and
     Emacsvox integrations in the buffer where you are working.

   The following sections introduce a practical starter set.  Use live
Help or the generated reference when you need the complete commands for
a particular application.

   ---------- Footnotes ----------

   (1) The prefix is inherited from Emacspeak, where C-e was mnemonic
for the project name.

   (2) Historically, d was mnemonic for DECtalk; the prefix now contains
engine-independent speech controls.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading,  Next: Speech System,  Prev: Working,  Up: Basic Usage

3.5 Reading And Navigating
==========================

Ordinary Emacs movement speaks the text crossed by point: moving by a
character, word, line, or paragraph presents the corresponding unit.
The commands below let you inspect useful units without first moving.
They use the default ‘C-e’ prefix.

   This is deliberately a starter set, not a second complete key
reference.  If a binding below differs in your session, use ‘C-h k’ on
that key and check your customizations.  The complete generated defaults
are in the *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps::.

‘C-e c’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-char’ speaks the character at point.
‘C-e w’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-word’ speaks the current word.  Repeating it at the
     same position spells the word.
‘C-e l’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-line’ speaks the current line.
‘C-e [’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-paragraph’ speaks the current paragraph.
‘C-e ]’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-page’ speaks the current page.
‘C-e r’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-region’ speaks the active region between point and
     mark.
‘C-e i’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-rest-of-buffer’ speaks from point to the end of the
     buffer.
‘C-e b’

     ‘emacsvox-speak-buffer’ speaks the entire current buffer.
‘C-e s’

     ‘tts-stop’ stops current speech.
‘C-e C-d’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-show-point’ toggles the aural indication of point
     within spoken text.

   Many speaking commands accept a prefix argument to limit speech to
the text before or after point.  Use ‘C-h k’ on a command before relying
on those variants, because their exact argument behavior is documented
with the command.


