3.6 Adjust Speech While You Work
================================

Start with the controls in this section when speech is too fast, too
detailed, or inappropriate for the current task.  Most settings apply
only to the current buffer.  Press ‘C-u’ before a command when its help
says that a prefix argument changes the default for all buffers.

   The generated *note (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps:: contains
the complete speech keymap, including specialist server and
text-chunking controls.

* Menu:

* Choose Typing Echo: Controlling Echo.  Choose character, word, and line feedback while typing.
* Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail: Speech Output Control.  Adjust rate and how textual detail is presented.
* Stop Or Restart Speech: Miscellaneous.  Stop, silence, restart, or identify the speech server.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Controlling Echo,  Next: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

3.6.1 Choose Typing Echo
------------------------

Character echo speaks each character you type.  Word echo speaks a word
when you finish it, and optional line echo speaks a completed line.
Toggle each kind independently to suit the buffer: character feedback
may help in a form where accuracy matters, while word echo is often more
fluent for prose.

   The commands below affect the current buffer.  Press ‘C-u’ first to
change the corresponding default and apply it to the current buffer.

‘C-e d k’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-character-echo’

     Toggle character echo.
‘C-e d w’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-word-echo’

     Toggle word echo.
‘C-e d l’

     ‘emacsvox-toggle-line-echo’

     Toggle line echo.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Output Control,  Next: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Controlling Echo,  Up: Speech System

3.6.2 Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail
-----------------------------------

Use these controls to match speech to the current material.  A faster
rate may suit familiar prose; full punctuation can help when inspecting
code or exact text.  Start with rate and punctuation, then change
capitalization or indentation feedback only when the distinction would
help your current task.

   The commands affect the current buffer unless their interactive help
describes a global prefix form.  Use ‘C-h k’ on a key before changing
the global default.

‘C-e d r’
     Enter a speech rate for the current buffer with ‘tts-set-rate’.
‘C-e d DIGIT’
     Choose one of ten predefined rates with ‘0’ through ‘9’.
‘C-e d p’
     Choose ‘some’, ‘all’, or ‘none’ punctuation with
     ‘tts-set-punctuations’.
‘C-e d s’
     Toggle splitting of mixed-capital identifiers into separate words.
‘C-e d c / C-e d C’
     Toggle capitalization indication with the lowercase key.  Use the
     uppercase key to preview and choose whether capitals use speech, a
     tone, both, a custom rule, or no added indication.
‘C-e d i / C-e d I’
     Toggle indentation feedback with the lowercase key.  Use the
     uppercase key to preview spoken-column and tonal choices.

   Character-rate scaling, text chunking, engine-specific controls, and
reusable presentation profiles are intentionally outside this starter
chapter.  Use live help for the current speech keymap and see *note
Aural Presentation:: when you want a complete presentation setup rather
than a temporary buffer change.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Miscellaneous,  Prev: Speech Output Control,  Up: Speech System

3.6.3 Stop Or Restart Speech
----------------------------

Stopping speech ends the current output without disabling future
feedback.  Quiet mode is useful when you temporarily want to keep
working without speech.  Restart the server after correcting a backend
problem or changing the selected synthesizer.

‘C-e s’
     Stop speech now with ‘tts-stop’.
‘C-e d q’
     Toggle quiet mode in the current buffer.  Press ‘C-u’ first to
     toggle it globally.
‘C-e C-s’
     Stop and restart the selected speech server.
‘C-e d V’
     Speak the selected adapter and its version information.

   Use ‘C-h m’ or the generated keymap reference for server selection,
repeated-pattern cleanup, text splitting, language selection, and direct
engine controls.  Those specialist settings are intentionally outside
this starter guide.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice-lock,  Next: Status Information,  Prev: Speech System,  Up: Basic Usage

3.7 Hear Structure With Voice Lock
==================================

Voice Lock is enabled automatically in supported buffers.  It maps faces
and other structural text properties to speech personalities, allowing
comments, strings, headings, links, and other elements to sound distinct
without adding words to the document.

   Try it in source code, Org, Info, or EWW.  Move between two
differently styled elements, such as a comment and a string or a heading
and a link.  Press ‘C-e d v’ to turn Voice Lock off in that buffer,
compare the same text, then press the key again to restore it.

   Use ‘C-e d C-v’ when you want to change Voice Lock globally.  Turning
it off removes this layer of voice changes; it does not silence ordinary
speech, semantic feedback, tones, or auditory icons.

   The exact personalities depend on the speech engine and active
presentation settings.  For an explanation of the separate Voice Lock,
face-presentation, and semantic-presentation layers, see *note Voice
Lock::.  Use *note Aural Presentation:: when you want to choose voices,
sounds, profiles, or other reusable presentation behavior.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Status Information,  Prev: Voice-lock,  Up: Basic Usage

