4 Speech Backends
*****************

Emacsvox needs a speech-server process.  For a new installation, use
*Omnivox*.  Omnivox is the server that Emacsvox talks to; the engine
that produces speech underneath it is platform-specific.  The normal
engine is eSpeak NG on GNU/Linux, AVSpeechSynthesizer on macOS, and
WinRT SpeechSynthesizer on Windows.

   Omnivox 1.7.0 is the minimum supported release for Emacsvox 2026.9.2.
A newer compatible Omnivox release may be used, but the binary examples
and guided WSL2 installer below pin the tested 1.7.0 artifacts exactly.

   For a first installation, follow the linear procedure in *note
Installation::.  Return here when you need platform-specific build
details, compatibility servers, or deeper diagnostics.  Omnivox build
internals, engine behavior, and versioned protocols are documented and
maintained in the Omnivox repository
(https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox).

* Menu:

* Supported Platform Routes::    Choose the maintained route for this machine.
* Install Omnivox From A Release Binary: Installing An Omnivox Release Binary.  Install the verified Omnivox 1.7.0 archive.
* Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2.  Use the matching verified Windows archive without a build toolchain.
* Trying Native Windows Emacsvox:: Evaluate native Windows Emacs and Omnivox with bounded checks.
* Installing Omnivox From Source:: Build when a release archive does not match the host.
* Building Omnivox For WSL2::    Stage the Windows runtime from WSL2.
* Selecting And Checking Omnivox:: Prove speech before starting Emacsvox.
* Compatibility Speech Servers:: Understand the retained direct adapters.
* Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership.  Locate failures and authoritative detail.


