4.6 Building Omnivox For WSL2
=============================

Use this advanced route when maintaining the reproducible Emacsvox
Windows bundle or its optional engine integrations.  Ordinary WSL2 users
should use the pre-built release in *note Installing An Omnivox Windows
Release Under WSL2::.  This target builds from source and currently
produces an x86-64 Windows runtime.

   Before running it, install or configure all of the following:

   • A WSL2 distribution with Windows interoperation enabled.  Both
     ‘powershell.exe’ and ‘wslpath’ must run directly from the WSL
     shell, and the Windows user's ‘LocalAppData’ directory must be
     writable.

   • A working Docker client and daemon with Linux/AMD64 container
     support.  The current WSL user must be able to run ‘docker’ without
     adding ‘sudo’ because the Make target invokes it directly.  Docker
     Desktop with WSL integration is one way to provide this, but it is
     not the only possible Docker setup.

   • Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer.  This means the Windows-only
     ".NET Framework", not merely a modern ‘dotnet’ SDK.  Visual Studio
     is not required.

   • In WSL, ‘git’, GNU ‘make’, ‘curl’, ‘unzip’, ‘sha256sum’, and the
     normal GNU shell utilities supplied by a standard distribution.
     The build also uses commands such as ‘awk’, ‘cmp’, ‘find’,
     ‘readlink’, ‘sed’, ‘sort’, and ‘xargs’.

   • Network access for the first Docker image build, Rust dependency
     download, and two checksum-pinned NuGet packages.  Later builds
     reuse the local Docker, Cargo, and .NET package caches.

   • Emacsvox and Omnivox Git checkouts.  By default they must be
     sibling directories named ‘emacsvox’ and ‘omnivox’, although
     maintainers can set ‘OMNIVOX_DIR’ to an alternate Omnivox checkout.
     The Omnivox checkout must be at the exact source commit recorded
     for the checksum-pinned Piper companion in
     ‘servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock’ when using the clean
     release target.  The preparation target rejects a different
     revision instead of combining unmatched native components.  The
     development target described below accepts current source by
     omitting Piper.  Both checkouts need enough free space for the
     Docker image, native and cross-compiled build products, and caches.

   You do _not_ need to install Rust, MinGW, CMake, Clang, libclang,
eSpeak NG, Roslyn, Visual Studio, or the .NET reference assemblies in
WSL.  The target pins or builds those inside its controlled toolchain.
Eloquence and DECtalk runtimes and the RHVoice runtime and voice data
are optional and are not needed for the normal Windows WinRT voice.

   The quickest prerequisite check is:

     cd /path/to/emacsvox
     make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain

   This is more than a presence check: on its first run it downloads the
pinned .NET compiler packages, builds the pinned Docker image, and
verifies the exact Rust, MinGW, libclang, Roslyn, and .NET Framework
versions.  A failure names the missing host command or unsupported
framework before the full Omnivox build is attempted.

   Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox as shown in the source-build section.
Then, from a clean Emacsvox checkout whose sibling Omnivox checkout also
has no tracked changes, run:

     cd /path/to/emacsvox
     make windows-omnivox

   This is the reproducible release path.  It builds and verifies a
pinned Windows-GNU executable and stages a content-addressed runtime
beneath ‘servers/omnivox-bin’.  Once the Piper lock identifies an
Omnivox revision that contains these integrations, the payload includes
the matching, checksum-pinned Piper companion, the RHVoice helper
without an RHVoice runtime, the Flite helper with its compiled CMU US
SLT voice, and the RuTTS helper with its built-in male and female
Russian voices.  It contains no Piper model and no RuLex component.  It
also copies the runnable payload to a Windows-local directory so native
voice and eSpeak data discovery do not pay the WSL filesystem cost.
Optional Eloquence, DECtalk, and RHVoice runtimes are not downloaded by
this target or required for normal WinRT speech.

   The build does not report success on file checks alone.  Its final
live gate starts the exact Windows-local runtime, discovers Flite's SLT
voice and both RuTTS voices, and requires each helper to synthesize a
non-empty WAV.  Repeat that gate without rebuilding by running ‘make
verify-windows-omnivox-live’.

   To register Piper, review a model and its model card, keep its
adjacent ‘.onnx.json’ or ‘.json’ configuration with it, and supply an
absolute path while staging:

     OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx make windows-omnivox

   The target copies the model and configuration into a separate,
content-addressed directory below Windows ‘LocalAppData’ and records
their hashes without bundling them into the runtime.  Omitting
‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’ installs the companion but leaves Piper
unregistered.  After a configured build, start a fresh speech process
and confirm that the physical voice is present:

     ./servers/omnivox --engine piper --list-voices

   In Emacsvox, restart both speech processes or restart Emacs, then
press ‘C-e d e’.  That command lists the engines reported by the live
process, so Piper appears only after the newly staged server has started
and successfully loaded its model and helper.

   Maintainers testing tracked changes should use the development route
in *note Integration Maintenance:: rather than weakening the
clean-release guard:

     OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice.dll \
       OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice/data \
       make windows-omnivox-dev
     ./servers/omnivox --engine flite --list-voices
     ./servers/omnivox --engine rutts --list-voices

   This route builds current Omnivox source, stages the RHVoice helper,
Flite with its SLT voice, and RuTTS 6.3.3 with its built-in male and
female Russian voices, and deliberately omits the older pinned Piper
companion.  RuTTS needs no separate installation; RuLex is not included.
RHVoice appears only after installing a compatible Windows ‘RHVoice.dll’
plus language and voice data.  When its library and data variables are
supplied to the development target, staging validates them and records
Windows path pointers beside that local runtime; the launcher restores
them for later fresh Emacsvox processes.  Also supply
‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG’ when the installation has a separate
configuration directory.  The library, data, and configuration remain
external and are not copied into the Omnivox payload.  Installation and
validation instructions are in Omnivox's checkout under
‘docs/RHVOICE.md’ and ‘docs/RUTTS.md’.  Restart both speech processes or
Emacs after restaging or changing those inputs, then use ‘C-e d e’ to
inspect the engines reported by the new process.

   Piper testing from a newer committed Omnivox revision uses the
separate ‘windows-omnivox-piper-dev’ target and its native Windows CI
artifact.  The target requires the archive's published SHA-256 and a
reviewed external model, and rejects a dirty or different Omnivox
checkout.  See *note Integration Maintenance:: for the complete command
and provenance checks.  This development path does not change the
companion pinned for reproducible releases.


