5 Work With The Audio Desktop
*****************************

The _audio desktop_ is the Emacsvox working environment.  It is not a
separate program or a spoken copy of a graphical desktop.  Emacs keeps
each document, application, and result in a buffer; its modes understand
the kind of information in that buffer; and Emacsvox presents that
context through speech, voice changes, sounds, and structured
navigation.

   You can begin without learning a new desktop model.  Open a file or
application normally, use ‘C-e m’ to hear the current buffer and mode,
and press ‘C-h m’ to discover what that buffer can do.  The rest of this
chapter explains why the same approach works for text, Web pages, mail,
shells, source code, and many other tasks.

* Menu:

* Desktop Objects::              Understand and move between Emacs buffers.
* Object Oriented Desktop::      Let major and minor modes interpret each kind of content.
* Context-Sensitive Interaction:: Use aural structure, mode commands, buffers, and search.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Desktop Objects,  Next: Object Oriented Desktop,  Up: Audio Desktop

5.1 Buffers Hold Your Work
==========================

A buffer is Emacs's working view of one piece of information.  A buffer
might contain a file you are editing, an Info manual, a directory
listing, a Web page, a mail search, a shell session, or a temporary Help
result.  Some buffers are connected to files; many useful application
buffers are not.

   The mode line identifies the selected buffer and its major mode.
Press ‘C-e m’ whenever you want to hear it.  Common buffer commands
include:

‘C-x b’
     Switch to another buffer by name.  Completion lets you narrow the
     choices instead of remembering the complete name.

‘C-x C-b’
     Open a buffer list.  In that list, use ‘C-h m’ to learn its
     navigation, selection, saving, and cleanup commands.

‘C-x k’
     Close a buffer that you no longer need.  Emacs asks before
     discarding unsaved file changes.

   Moving to another buffer changes the selected work object without
closing the one you left.  This makes it practical to keep a document,
its reference material, a shell, and related application results
available together.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Object Oriented Desktop,  Next: Context-Sensitive Interaction,  Prev: Desktop Objects,  Up: Audio Desktop

5.2 Let Modes Give Each Buffer Its Tools
========================================

Every buffer has one major mode that provides its main editing or
application behavior.  A source buffer uses a programming-language mode,
Dired uses a mode for directories, EWW uses one for Web pages, and Org
uses one for structured notes and documents.  Minor modes add optional
behavior alongside the major mode.

   This context is what makes Emacs efficient: a movement command can
move by a sentence in prose, a heading in an outline, an item in a list,
or another meaningful unit supplied by the active mode.  Emacsvox speaks
the result of the operation and can use the same context to announce
state changes.

   You do not need to predict which commands every mode provides.  Press
‘C-h m’ in the buffer you are using, then ‘C-h k’ followed by a key when
you want a fuller explanation.  *Note Using Online Help:: provides a
short practical guide to these discovery commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Context-Sensitive Interaction,  Prev: Object Oriented Desktop,  Up: Audio Desktop

5.3 Hear And Navigate The Current Context
=========================================

Emacsvox uses information already exposed by Emacs and the active
application.  That produces more useful feedback than reading every
visible character in the same way.  You can hear what an object means,
move using its structure, and ask for detail only when you need it.

5.3.1 Hear Structure Without Extra Words
----------------------------------------

Voice changes can distinguish headings, comments, strings, links,
warnings, and other meaningful text.  Short sounds and tones can mark
events or state changes without interrupting the spoken content.
Ordinary text remains available through the usual reading commands.

   The exact result depends on the mode, the Emacsvox integration, and
your Aural Presentation settings.  *Note Aural Presentation:: explains
how the parts fit together, including the established Voice Lock
compatibility layer; ‘C-e E’ explains the presentation chosen for the
object at point.

5.3.2 Move By The Structure Of The Content
------------------------------------------

Prefer the navigation supplied by the active mode when it matches your
task.  For example, Info moves among nodes and menu entries, Org and
Outline move among headings, Dired moves among files, programming modes
understand definitions and diagnostics, and EWW exposes links and
document structure.

   Begin with ‘C-h m’.  It describes the commands available in the
running buffer, including personal customizations and installed
packages.  *Note Browsing Structure:: points to maintained guides for
several common types of content.

5.3.3 Move Between Tasks Without Losing Your Place
--------------------------------------------------

Buffers keep different tasks available in one Emacs session.  Use ‘C-x
b’ to return to a known buffer or ‘C-x C-b’ to browse the current set.
Emacs windows can show more than one buffer at a time; if the selected
window is unclear, ‘C-e C-w’ describes the arrangement.

   Applications often create several related buffers, such as a search
result and an individual message.  Their mode help explains the commands
for moving among those views.  Treat each buffer as a place you can
revisit, rather than as a dialog that must be completed before doing
anything else.

5.3.4 Search Wherever You Are
-----------------------------

In most text buffers, ‘C-s’ searches forward incrementally and ‘C-r’
searches backward.  Type more text to narrow the match, repeat the
search key to move to another match, and press ‘C-g’ to cancel.  This
same basic search is available in documents, Help, source code, and many
application results.

   Also look for the mode's semantic search and filtering commands.  A
mail application can search message fields, Dired can narrow files, and
a browser can move directly among links or headings.  Use the general
search when text is what matters and the mode-specific command when the
kind of object matters.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Aural Presentation,  Next: Using Online Help,  Prev: Audio Desktop,  Up: Top

6 Aural Presentation: Speech, Voices, And Sound
***********************************************

Aural Presentation is the Emacsvox system that decides _how_ information
and events should be heard.  It can combine spoken text, voice changes,
auditory icons, tones, pauses, and spatial cues in one ordered
presentation.  The speech server delivers that result; it does not
decide what a heading, unread message, completed command, warning, or
other object means.

   You do not need to configure Aural Presentation before using
Emacsvox.  The built-in baseline and application integrations provide
useful defaults.  When you do want a change, start from the object you
heard and use Aural Home rather than editing configuration data by hand:

‘C-e H’
     Open Aural Home, the spoken control center for understanding,
     adjusting, and diagnosing presentation.

‘C-e E’
     Explain why the object at point received its voices, sounds, and
     other actions.

   This chapter gives a user-level model of the system and practical
workflows for common changes.  The generated reference remains the
authority for rule schemas, transport contracts, and extension
development.

* Menu:

* Presentation Model::           Follow meaning from an Emacs application to audible output.
* Presentation Modalities::      Recognize speech, voices, cues, tones, pauses, and spatial output.
* Aural Home::                   Navigate the spoken presentation control center.
* Explaining Presentation::      Explain current or recently queued feedback.
* Adjusting Presentation::       Preview, scope, save, and undo presentation changes.
* Presentation Profiles::        Save and switch complete presentation configurations.
* Voices And Routing::           Distinguish logical styles, palettes, physical voices, and engines.
* Sounds And Space::             Audition sound packs and inspect spatial fallback.
* Presentation Troubleshooting:: Trace unexpected output and use safe diagnostics.
* Voice Lock Compatibility: Voice Lock.  Understand the established face and personality voice adapter.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Model,  Next: Presentation Modalities,  Up: Aural Presentation

6.1 A Practical Model Of Presentation
=====================================

It helps to separate four questions that are often mixed together:

Layer            Question               Example
Meaning          What happened or       A level-two heading was reached
                 what is this?          during navigation.
Presentation     What should convey     Speak the heading in a named
                 that meaning?          voice and play a section cue
                                        first.
Resources        Which voice and        Resolve the logical voice through
                 sound realize that     the active palette and the cue
                 request?               through the active sound pack.
Output           What can this          Route speech to an installed
                 machine produce?       engine and apply supported
                                        spatial behavior.

   An Emacs mode or Emacsvox integration supplies facts about an object
or event.  Facts can describe a role such as a heading, an event such as
completion, a state such as unread, or an attribute such as heading
level.  Context also says whether you are navigating, reading
continuously, or receiving a state change.  This allows the same object
to receive concise feedback during continuous reading and more
orientation when you move to it deliberately.

   Presentation rules match those facts and context.  A rule can choose
a voice, add speech or a cue, insert a pause or tone, request spatial
placement, or suppress something that is unhelpful.  Several matching
rules can compose; a stronger, narrower rule need change only the
relevant part rather than repeat the complete presentation.

   The user-visible configuration layers are:

  1. A fixed internal baseline that preserves established Emacsvox
     behavior.

  2. Automatic integration behavior supplied by the active mode or
     package.

  3. Enabled _Presentation Options_ that add optional behavior in a
     stable weakest-to-strongest order.

  4. Personal, session, and current-buffer _overrides_.  These are the
     strongest layers and are intended for precise changes.

   A presentation profile is not another rule layer.  It records a
complete set of selected options, sound pack, voice palette, and spatial
choices so you can switch those choices together.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Modalities,  Next: Aural Home,  Prev: Presentation Model,  Up: Aural Presentation

6.2 What You Can Hear
=====================

Aural Presentation can use one or more of these forms of feedback:

   • _Speech_ conveys content, labels, prompts, results, and status.
     Added speech should explain meaning that is not already clear from
     the content.

   • _Voice_ changes characteristics of spoken content.  Named logical
     voices make distinctions such as headings, annotations, warnings,
     or quoted text portable across speech engines.

   • _Auditory cues_ are short recognizable sounds associated with
     semantic events or objects.  A sound pack supplies the concrete
     audio for those cue names.

   • _Tones_ convey compact information using a named pitch and
     duration.  They are useful when a short distinction is preferable
     to speech or a sampled sound.

   • _Pauses_ separate related parts of a presentation without adding
     words.

   • _Spatial presentation_ can place speech or cues across supported
     output.  When the selected backend cannot provide the requested
     placement, Emacsvox keeps the content audible using its documented
     fallback.

   These forms can be ordered around the content.  For example, entering
a section might play a cue, speak the heading in a distinctive voice,
and leave a short pause before body text.  The presentation is one
result, not several unrelated notifications.

   The precise sound may vary by speech engine, installed voices, sound
pack, and audio hardware.  The intended distinction should remain
understandable even when a simpler backend uses fewer voice
characteristics or centered output.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Aural Home,  Next: Explaining Presentation,  Prev: Presentation Modalities,  Up: Aural Presentation

6.3 Begin With Aural Home
=========================

Open Aural Home from an ordinary buffer with ‘C-e H’.  Emacsvox
remembers that source buffer so contextual actions such as explanation
and remapping can operate on the object you were using.

   Aural Home is a spoken table.  Each row names an area, reports its
current status, and describes its purpose.  The common controls are:

‘n, p, down, up’
     Move by row and speak the new row.
‘left, right’
     Move among the titled columns.
‘.’
     Speak the current column title and value.
‘SPC’
     Speak the complete current row.
‘RET’
     Open or perform the current row's main action.
‘?’
     Display and speak help for the current Aural interface.
‘h’
     Return to Aural Home from a manager or editor.
‘q’
     Leave the current Aural interface.

   The rows fall into a few practical groups:

Goal                Aural Home routes
Understand          Explain At Point, Recent Aural Feedback, Semantic
feedback            Vocabulary, and Training Mode.
Change one          Remap Voice At Point, Remap Earcon At Point,
behavior            Presentation Overrides, and Current Buffer Rules.
Select broader      Presentation Options and Presentation Profiles.
behavior
Adjust resources    Voice Palettes, Voice Workbench, Sound Packs,
                    Spatial Capabilities, and Spatial Settings.
Diagnose the        Aural Doctor.
system

   You can press <RET> on a row without memorizing its shortcut.  Aural
Home also provides direct keys for frequent routes, including ‘x’ for
explanation, ‘O’ for overrides, ‘H’ for recent feedback, ‘P’ for
profiles, ‘V’ for voice palettes, ‘W’ for Voice Workbench, and ‘D’ for
Aural Doctor.


