6.5 Make A Small Change Safely
==============================

Start by choosing the narrowest tool that matches your intention:

Intention                      Use                    Scope
Try an optional behavior       Presentation Options   Global
supplied by Emacsvox or an                            configuration
integration
Change the voice or earcon     Remap At Point         Personal, session,
for the object at point                               or current buffer
Review or remove an existing   Presentation           Its recorded scope
precise change                 Overrides
Try rules only in the          Current Buffer Rules   Life of that
current work buffer                                   buffer
Switch a complete reading or   Presentation           Saved global
working setup                  Profiles               configuration

6.5.1 Remap One Item At Point
-----------------------------

  1. Move to a representative object and press ‘C-e E’ so you know what
     currently controls it.

  2. Press ‘C-e H’.  Use ‘r’ to remap its voice or ‘R’ to remap one
     exact earcon.

  3. Choose the scope deliberately:

        • a _personal_ override persists across Emacs sessions;
        • a _session_ override lasts until this Emacs exits; and
        • a _buffer_ override affects only the remembered live buffer.

  4. Review the generated rule in the editor.  It is initially
     unwritten.  Use the editor's ‘?’ help, preview when offered, and
     press ‘w’ only when the target, scope, and change are correct.

  5. Return to the source, repeat the action, and use ‘C-e E’ again to
     confirm which rule now wins.

   Open _Presentation Overrides_ to manage these changes together.
There, ‘P’ previews the complete current result, ‘t’ temporarily
disables or enables a rule, and ‘d’ removes it.  Disabling or deleting
an override restores the next weaker behavior; it does not leave a
copied default behind.

6.5.2 Try A Presentation Option
-------------------------------

Presentation Options are maintained additions such as richer heading
feedback or integration-specific cues.  From Aural Home, open
_Presentation Options_:

  1. Use <TAB> or <RET> on a collection to expand it.

  2. Move to an option and press ‘P’ to preview it composed with the
     current configuration.  Press ‘C-u P’ to preview it in isolation.

  3. Press ‘t’ to enable or disable it.  Press ‘a’ to hear enabled
     options in their exact weakest-to-strongest order.

  4. Return to the source and test the original task.  Personal,
     session, and buffer overrides remain stronger than an enabled
     option.

   Avoid reordering options merely to solve one exceptional object.  A
narrow override usually expresses that intention more clearly.


