6.6 Save Complete Setups With Profiles
======================================

A presentation profile records a coordinated setup: enabled Presentation
Options and their order, the sound pack, the voice palette, and portable
spatial settings.  This is useful for switching between focused work and
leisure reading, between quiet and information-rich feedback, or between
audio environments.

   To save and use a profile:

  1. Configure the live options, sound pack, voice palette, and spatial
     choices you want.

  2. Open _Presentation Profiles_ from Aural Home or press ‘P’ there.

  3. Press ‘N’ to save the current live configuration as a new named
     profile.

  4. Later, select that profile and press <RET> or ‘a’ to apply its
     saved values.

  5. If you deliberately change the live configuration and want the
     selected profile to adopt it, press ‘w’.  If you instead want to
     discard the live differences, press ‘a’ to restore the saved
     profile; Emacsvox asks before replacing the diverged values.

   Only one profile identity is selected.  _Active_ means the selected
profile's saved values match the live configuration.  _Diverged_ means
the same profile is still selected but the live values differ; it is a
status, not an unsaved editor warning.

   Profiles do not copy presentation rules.  Edit rules in Presentation
Options or Overrides.  They also do not capture buffer-local Voice Lock
state or current-buffer rules, because those belong to an individual
buffer rather than a global setup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voices And Routing,  Next: Sounds And Space,  Prev: Presentation Profiles,  Up: Aural Presentation

6.7 Choose Voices And Speech Engines
====================================

Four terms help when adjusting voices:

   • A _logical voice_ expresses a purpose such as boldening,
     annotation, or a heading distinction without naming one synthesizer
     voice.

   • A _voice palette_ gives named logical voices portable style
     characteristics.  Palettes can inherit from another palette and
     travel between machines.

   • A _physical voice_ is an actual voice reported by an installed
     speech engine.  Its exact identifier is usually machine-local.

   • An _engine route_ decides which engine and physical voice realize a
     logical voice on the current machine.

   Use the _Voice Palettes_ manager when you want to choose or edit the
portable style vocabulary.  Press <RET> on a palette to browse its
voices, preview them before editing, and use ‘a’ in the palette manager
to activate a selected palette.  Built-in palettes remain intact;
editing can create a personal copy.

   Use _Voice Workbench_ when the question involves installed voices,
engines, or routes.  Open it from Aural Home with ‘W’.  Its main views
are:

‘l’
     Logical voices and their current routes.
‘v’
     Physical voices reported by the engines.
‘e’
     Engines, preference, fallback, and health.
‘s’
     Portable styles and effects.

   Press ‘P’ to preview a row, ‘A’ to preview all visible voices, ‘B’ to
compare two, and ‘S’ to stop preview.  Use ‘a’ to assign a physical
voice and ‘t’ to tune the selected logical voice on its effective route.
Route changes are staged until you press ‘w’ or ‘C-c C-c’ to save and
apply them.  Pressing ‘q’ with unsaved route changes warns that they
remain staged.

   The tuner names neutral effect values instead of speaking an
ambiguous number: gain five is _unchanged_, low-pass nine is _disabled_,
high-pass zero is _disabled_, pan five is _centre_, and reverb, echo, or
chorus zero is _disabled_.  Chorus adds a bounded doubled-voice
modulation without changing speech duration or marker positions.  It is
applied by Omnivox to buffered audio; the tuner reports when the
selected route cannot apply it.  These portable levels are translated to
the exact values required by the active adapter.

   The built-in ‘acss-default’ palette also includes a ‘telephone’
logical voice.  It combines high-pass and low-pass filtering to give any
buffered Omnivox engine a narrow-band telephone sound while preserving
its selected physical voice, duration, and markers.  Preview it in
_Voice Palettes_ or _Voice Workbench_ before assigning it to content.
Adapters without post-synthesis filtering preserve the portable style
but do not apply the filters.

   Omnivox average-pitch differences use the customizable
‘omnivox-average-pitch-contrast’.  Its default value, ‘0.5’, keeps
palette distinctions while making them gentler.  Set it to ‘1.0’ for the
historical contrast, or ‘0’ to make average pitch neutral.  Values
through ‘2.0’ are accepted.  Changing it refreshes logical-voice
registration, and the same setting applies to previews, structured
speech, and legacy inline voices.

   For a quick engine preference, ‘C-e d e’ chooses an available engine
for the current Emacs session.  Press ‘C-u’ first to save the new
preference.  On a first run with no saved routing profile, the ordinary
command uses the live Omnivox policy as an in-memory baseline and writes
no file; the prefixed command explicitly saves that baseline and the
selected preference.  Explicit logical-voice routes still take
precedence.

   Judge voices in context.  The goal is usually to distinguish nearby
kinds of content, not to identify every named voice in isolation.  Use a
portable palette for the intended distinction and machine routing for
the best local realization.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Sounds And Space,  Next: Presentation Troubleshooting,  Prev: Voices And Routing,  Up: Aural Presentation

6.8 Choose Sounds And Spatial Cues
==================================

Presentation rules refer to auditory cues by semantic name.  A sound
pack maps those names to concrete audio assets and can inherit missing
cues from another pack.  This separation lets you change the overall
sound character without rewriting every presentation rule.

   Open _Sound Packs_ from Aural Home.  In the manager:

‘P’
     Audition a representative cue without activating the pack.
‘RET’
     Browse the pack's individual cues and their source or fallback.
‘P or RET in the cue browser’
     Audition the selected concrete cue.
‘v’
     Validate the pack and report missing or invalid resources.
‘a’
     Activate the selected pack.

   Use a sound pack when you want to change a family of cues together.
Use _Remap Earcon At Point_ or Presentation Overrides when one semantic
event needs a different cue, suppression, or restoration.

   Spatial requests are portable descriptions rather than assumptions
about one device.  Aural Home separates _Spatial Capabilities_, which
reports what the current route can provide and how it falls back, from
_Spatial Settings_, which controls output, separation, direction,
speech, and cues.  Unsupported or mono output remains audible at the
center rather than silently losing content.

   Named tones are another compact presentation resource.  They do not
require a sampled sound file and may be used independently of the active
sound pack.


