8.1 Document Authoring
======================

Emacs major modes provide the editing commands for each document format.
Emacsvox speaks ordinary movement and editing, applies voice-lock where
the mode exposes useful faces, and adds mode-specific feedback where an
integration exists.  Open or create the document in its normal Emacs
mode, then use ‘C-h m’ to discover that mode's workflow.  For outlines
and templates, see *note Structured Editing::.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Structured Editing,  Next: Browsing Structure,  Prev: Document Authoring,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.2 Structured Editing And Templates
====================================

Emacsvox has maintained integrations for the built-in Outline and Tempo
libraries.  In Outline mode, heading navigation speaks the reached
heading and visibility commands announce what was hidden or exposed.
Start a dedicated outline with ‘M-x outline-mode’, or enable
‘outline-minor-mode’ in a suitable document.  See *note
(emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-outline:: for the Emacsvox integration and
*note (emacs)Outline Mode:: for the underlying editor.

   Tempo supplies template fields for modes and packages that use it.
Emacsvox speaks inserted text and the line reached when moving between
template marks.  The package that supplied a template also provides its
insertion commands; use that package's mode help, together with *note
(emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-tempo::.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Browsing Structure,  Next: Web Browsing,  Prev: Structured Editing,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.3 Browsing Structure
======================

Use the structure exposed by the active Emacs mode rather than treating
every buffer as plain text.  Info provides node and menu navigation,
Outline provides heading and visibility commands, and EWW exposes HTML
headings, links, lists, tables, and other document elements.  Begin with
‘C-h m’ in the buffer; see *note Using Online Help:: for the common
discovery commands and *note Web Browsing:: for the maintained Web
route.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Web Browsing,  Next: Messaging,  Prev: Browsing Structure,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.4 Web Browsing With EWW
=========================

EWW is the built-in browser supported for current Emacsvox.  Run ‘M-x
eww <RET>’, enter a URL or search words, and press <RET>.  Emacsvox
speaks EWW navigation and rendering feedback and adds commands for
moving by HTML structure and filtering the displayed DOM.  In an EWW
buffer, use ‘C-h m’ for the live workflow and ‘C-h b’ for its effective
keys.  See *note (emacs)EWW:: for browser operation and *note
(emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-eww:: for the speech integration.

   The former Emacs/W3 and w3m survey is historical.  Do not install
either one on the strength of the Heritage manual; use EWW unless a
separately maintained integration says otherwise.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Messaging,  Next: Editing Code,  Prev: Web Browsing,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.5 Messaging
=============

Emacsvox integrates with several Emacs mail and messaging modes.  The
curated workflow for indexed mail search, asynchronous results, thread
reading, attachments, and privacy controls is *note Notmuch Mail::.  For
another client, start with its own manual and ‘C-h m’; use the generated
reference to find an Emacsvox module with the same package or mode name.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Editing Code,  Next: Development Environment,  Prev: Messaging,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.6 Editing Code
================

Programming modes provide language-aware movement, indentation,
compilation, and navigation.  Emacsvox speaks those operations and uses
voice-lock to distinguish faces such as comments, strings, warnings, and
language constructs.  Use ‘C-h m’ in the source buffer for the language
mode, and search the generated reference for an integration named after
that mode.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Development Environment,  Next: Desktop Management,  Prev: Editing Code,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.7 Development Environment
===========================

Emacs can combine compilation, diagnostics, project navigation, version
control, debuggers, and language servers into a development environment.
Emacsvox supplies speech feedback to many of those components, but their
own manuals remain authoritative for setup and operation.  Verify each
component individually with its mode help before combining it into a
larger workflow.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Desktop Management,  Next: Personal Information Management,  Prev: Development Environment,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.8 Desktop Management
======================

Emacs buffers, windows, frames, and tabs provide the workspace.  Their
standard commands receive speech feedback, and the active help describes
any additional mode-specific bindings.  Use ‘C-x b’ to select a buffer
and ‘C-h m’ in specialized buffer-list or tab interfaces to learn their
local commands.


