8.9 Personal Information Management
===================================

Calendar, Org, and related packages define their data formats and
workflows.  Emacsvox integrations present navigation, state changes, and
structured content when those modes are active.  Begin with the
application's own manual and live mode help, then consult its matching
Emacsvox module in the generated reference.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Desktop Applications,  Prev: Personal Information Management,  Up: Emacs Packages

8.10 Desktop Applications
=========================

8.10.1 Terminal Applications
----------------------------

For a new full-terminal workflow, use *note EAT Terminal Access::.  When
a built-in terminal with no additional package is preferable, use *note
Running Terminal Based Applications::.  For ordinary line-oriented shell
commands, ‘M-x shell’ is usually the simplest route.

8.10.2 Forms Mode
-----------------

The built-in Forms library presents records from a structured text file
one at a time.  Emacsvox speaks field values, record movement, searches,
and status changes while Forms mode is active.  Forms requires a format
specification; see *note (emacs)Forms Mode:: for setup and operation,
and *note (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-forms:: for the speech
integration.

8.10.3 OCR And PDF-To-Text With PaddleOCR
-----------------------------------------

Emacsvox uses PaddleOCR PP-StructureV3 to turn an image or PDF into
UTF-8 Markdown text.  PP-StructureV3 recovers document reading order and
represents recognized tables as Markdown or embedded HTML, so keep the
‘.md’ output when table structure matters.  Markdown is plain text and
can instead be named ‘.txt’ when its lightweight markup is acceptable.

   The supported baseline below is CPU-only x86-64 WSL2 or GNU/Linux.
Paddle's Linux wheel requires an AVX-capable x86-64 processor and Python
3.9 through 3.13.  In particular, do not use Ubuntu 26.04's Python 3.14
for this environment.  The commands install a separate Python 3.13 so
that they do not alter Emacsvox, system Python, or another project's
packages.  The initial installation uses about 1.7 GB.  The first
complete PP-StructureV3 run downloads approximately another 1.8 GB of
models; later runs reuse that cache.

   Follow the official uv installation guide
(https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/) if command
‘uv’ is not already available, then create the isolated environment:

     uv python install 3.13
     uv venv --python 3.13 --seed \
       "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv"

   Install the tested CPU runtime and the PaddleOCR ‘all’ variant.  The
‘all’ extra includes the document-parsing dependencies needed by
PP-StructureV3, including table and formula support.

     "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv/bin/python" -m pip install \
       paddlepaddle==3.3.0 \
       -i https://www.paddlepaddle.org.cn/packages/stable/cpu/
     "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv/bin/python" -m pip install \
       'paddleocr[all]==3.7.0'

   From the Emacsvox checkout, verify that the launcher finds both
packages:

     ./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --check

   The expected line names PaddlePaddle 3.3.0, PaddleOCR 3.7.0, and the
CPU device.  A warning that ‘ccache’ is absent is harmless for normal
inference.  oneDNN, formerly called MKL-DNN, is Paddle's optional
optimized CPU execution backend; it is not an OCR model.  The launcher
deliberately leaves it disabled because the stable PaddlePaddle 3.3.0
CPU runtime has an upstream PIR-to-oneDNN conversion defect
(https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/Paddle/issues/77340).  In the tested
pipeline the defect aborts initialization when PP-StructureV3 reaches
its formula model, but the model itself is not at fault.  With oneDNN
disabled, Paddle uses its standard CPU kernels and every recognition
feature remains enabled, including tables and formulas.  The trade-off
is speed, not recognition capability.

   To recognize one image at the shell, redirect the clean Markdown
result to a file.  Paddle downloads its models on the first real command
and writes progress messages to standard error without mixing them into
the recognized text.

     ./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr "/path/to/page.png" > page.md

   PDF input uses the same command.  Paddle renders and recognizes every
page, then joins the pages in reading order:

     ./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr "/path/to/scanned-document.pdf" > document.md

   This route is intended for scanned or image-only PDFs.  For a
born-digital PDF that already has a trustworthy text layer, extracting
that layer is faster and usually more exact than OCR.  OCR quality still
depends on scan quality; 300-DPI, upright, high-contrast source pages
are a good starting point.  A large PDF can take substantial CPU time
because table, layout, formula, orientation, and unwarping models are
all active by default.

   Within Emacsvox, press ‘C-e C-o’ or run ‘M-x emacsvox-ocr <RET>’.  In
the OCR buffer:

   • Press ‘o’, choose an image or PDF, and wait for the completion cue.
     OCR runs asynchronously, and the result appears as a new page in
     the buffer.
   • Press <SPC> to read the current result, ‘s’ to save it, or ‘w’ to
     save the complete OCR buffer.  Press ‘[’ and ‘]’ to move between
     results.
   • Press ‘d’ to open the per-user OCR directory.  Engine progress and
     errors are kept separately in buffer ‘*Emacsvox OCR Errors*’.

   For ordinary use inside Emacs, customize OCR in Emacs rather than
setting shell environment variables.  Run ‘M-x customize-group <RET>
emacsvox-ocr <RET>’.  The bundled adapter has typed options for its
normal choices:

   • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-python’ selects an exact Python executable
     when the default isolated environment is not appropriate.  Leave it
     at ‘nil’ for the installation path used above.
   • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-language’ and
     ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-device’ select the recognition language and
     Paddle device.
   • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-table-recognition’ and
     ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-formula-recognition’ control the
     structural models.  Both are enabled by default.
   • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-document-orientation’,
     ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-document-unwarping’, and
     ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-text-line-orientation’ control image
     correction.
   • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-enable-mkldnn’ enables oneDNN/MKL-DNN
     acceleration.  It does not enable or disable any OCR model.  Keep
     it disabled for the tested PaddlePaddle 3.3.0 CPU setup on WSL.

   The PaddleOCR options apply only while ‘emacsvox-ocr-engine’ names
the bundled adapter.  Selecting a different engine preserves the generic
engine interface.  ‘emacsvox-ocr-engine-options’ remains an advanced
list of literal arguments; for the bundled adapter it follows, and can
override, the typed options.

   The environment variables are retained for direct shell or automation
use of ‘bin/emacsvox-paddleocr’, not as the primary Emacs configuration
interface.  ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_VENV’ changes the launcher's environment
directory, ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_PYTHON’ selects an exact Python
executable, and ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_LANGUAGE’ or
‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_DEVICE’ changes the standalone defaults.  See
‘./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --help’ for command-line switches.

   The direct SANE scanner-acquisition commands are retained for
compatibility but are not part of this tested WSL baseline.  It is
usually simpler to scan with a Windows application and choose the
resulting image or PDF with ‘o’.  Scanner configuration is independent
of PaddleOCR.


