16.3 Integration Maintenance
============================

This chapter collects source-level contracts that are deliberately
omitted from the user task guides.  Optional package APIs and renderers
evolve, so recheck the upstream interfaces and keep compatibility
behavior bounded when changing an integration.

16.3.1 Source checkout and byte-code lifecycle
----------------------------------------------

Use the Emacs selected by the ignored repository-local ‘local.mk’.  When
Emacs 31 or newer is not the system ‘emacs’, set its absolute path
there:

     EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs

   Run ‘make check-emacs’ before diagnosing compilation.  After an
ordinary Lisp edit, use the incremental build:

     make bytecode

   After switching branches, pulling changes, or changing a macro or
public interface, use:

     make bytecode-rebuild

   That target removes ignored generated ‘.elc’ files and recompiles
them with the selected Emacs; it does not clean or discard source
changes.  ‘make bytecode-check’ is the non-mutating preflight for
startup or deployment.  It reports missing, stale, orphaned, or
wrong-Emacs byte-code.

   Tests deliberately prefer source and therefore do not prove that the
byte-code used by a live profile is current.  Verify rebuilt byte-code
in a fresh Emacs rather than relying on a file reload when compiled
dependants may already be resident.

   During development, load the changing setup source explicitly:

     (load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")

   The setup entry point warns and prefers source when newer Lisp would
otherwise be hidden by stale local byte-code.  In a stable checkout with
current ‘.elc’ files, ‘(load-library "emacsvox-setup")’ may be used.  Do
not use ‘load-library’ as the first load after editing the source tree:
Emacs can select an old ‘emacsvox-setup.elc’ before the newer source
checker runs.

16.3.2 Emacsvox release staging and publication
-----------------------------------------------

The repository-level ‘VERSION’ file is the canonical stable release
identifier.  Calendar-versioning policy, including delayed patches and
backports, is recorded in ADR 0002
(../adr/0002-calendar-versioning.org).  When advancing a release, update
‘VERSION’, the literal package header in ‘lisp/emacsvox.el’, and the
current heading in ‘etc/NEWS’ together, then run:

     make version-check

   Between releases, ‘etc/NEWS’ uses the ‘Emacsvox Unreleased’ heading,
so changes can be recorded before deciding whether the next publication
is a patch or a new feature-release series.  After publishing, preserve
those notes verbatim as ‘etc/NEWS-VERSION’ and start a new unreleased
file.  Ordinary ‘version-check’ accepts that state; ‘release-check’,
‘release-tag’, and ‘release-publish’ require the current NEWS heading to
match ‘VERSION’ exactly.

   Prepare an artifact only from a separate clean, committed worktree.
Build fresh byte-code and run the guarded artifact target:

     make bytecode-rebuild
     make release-artifact

   ‘release-artifact’ runs the complete test and documentation gates
before creating ‘dist/emacsvox-YYYY.MONTH.PATCH.tar.bz2’, its SHA-256
checksum, and a provenance record naming the checked source commit.  For
a patch-zero feature release, the series must match the current month.
A corrective or backport release retains its original non-future series
as required by ADR 0002.  Inspect the archive, checksum, and provenance
before proceeding.

   Stop after artifact inspection unless fresh explicit permission names
the release to be tagged and published.  Preparing an artifact,
committing or pushing a branch, publishing documentation, or receiving a
general instruction to continue is not permission to create or push a
release tag.  Once that separate authorization has been given, the
guarded commands are:

     make release-tag
     make release-publish

   ‘release-tag’ validates the artifact against the still-clean ‘HEAD’
and creates a local annotated, unsigned tag; it does not push the tag.
The unsigned-tag policy and its trust boundary are recorded in ADR 0006
(../adr/0006-annotated-unsigned-release-tags.org).  ‘release-publish’
repeats the artifact and source-commit validation, pushes only that
exact tag, and creates the GitHub release in the repository identified
by ‘RELEASE_REMOTE’.  This explicit selection prevents the GitHub CLI
from choosing an ‘upstream’ remote when releasing from a fork.  No
release target pushes ‘master’ or the documentation branch.  Use ‘make
release-check’ alone when a non-mutating preflight is wanted without
creating an artifact.

16.3.3 Future Aural contract decisions
--------------------------------------

The completed Aural implementation diary is preserved under
‘archive/emacsvox/migrations/’ rather than serving as current
instructions.  Three questions from that diary remain deliberately
undecided and require an explicit architecture decision before
implementation:

   • whether a critical alert may be completely suppressed or must
     retain an alternative presentation;
   • which deterministic ordering policy would apply before minor-mode
     selectors can participate in the presentation cascade; and
   • whether a new presentation occasion belongs to the core semantic
     contract or to a module-owned extension.

   Do not infer answers from the archived proposals.  Current behavior
is defined by the Aural implementation, its generated reference, and
accepted ADRs.

16.3.4 Notmuch contracts
------------------------

The live module is registered as an optional package integration.  Its
stable extension points are the Hello, Search, Show, and Tree mode
hooks; Search and Show mode maps; public interactive commands; and
public Search/Show accessors.  Advice is installed only when a target
exists and is target-aware, so a nested or programmatic upstream call
does not claim the outer user's feedback.

   The Show landing and visibility paths also read renderer metadata
such as ‘:headers-overlay’, ‘:message-visible’, message extents,
‘:notmuch-part’, part-hidden state, and Notmuch part-button types.
Missing optional metadata should reduce presentation detail rather than
authorize an operation.  Review these adapters whenever the Notmuch
renderer changes.

   Each user-owned asynchronous Search process carries its own
completion state as a process property.  The upstream sentinel renders
first; Emacsvox then clears that property before delivering exactly one
terminal result.  The owning buffer and process status are
authoritative.  A killed owner remains silent, concurrent buffers cannot
overwrite one another, and the global refresh-all path intentionally
acquires no ownership.

   Run ‘make notmuch-test’ for the explicit source suite.  After current
byte-code exists, run ‘make compiled-notmuch-test’; it verifies the
exact loaded ‘emacsvox-notmuch.elc’, compiler version, and the same
focused selector, including real displayed-window reading for both
advance paths.

16.3.5 Agent Shell contracts
----------------------------

Use ‘M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable’ to reinstall speech hooks after
deliberately disabling them or while testing live integration changes.

   The integration uses three deliberately different contract layers:

Layer         Agent Shell owner           Emacsvox rule
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Public        ‘agent-shell-subscribe-to’, Primary authority for
structured    ‘agent-shell-unsubscribe’,  processing, completion,
API           ‘agent-shell-status’, and   errors, permission urgency,
              ‘agent-shell-shell-buffer’; and tool status.  The public
              lifecycle, permission,      turn boundary triggers
              and tool events             inspection of renderer-owned
                                          response sections.  Event and
                                          response data is treated as
                                          untrusted content and bounded
                                          before automatic Aural
                                          submission.
Experimental  ‘agent-shell-ui-state’,     Feature-detected navigation
renderer      ‘agent-shell-ui-section’,   and presentation input.
metadata      rendered Markdown           Renderer group provenance
              table/source properties,    outranks provider-controlled
              and renderer-owned faces    IDs; prose and IDs cannot
                                          manufacture structured
                                          permission or lifecycle
                                          authority.
Private       The aggregate               Kept in narrow adapter
compatibility ‘agent-shell--state’, the   functions for older Agent
fallback      legacy viewport ownership   Shell releases or incomplete
              resolver, viewport          renderers.  A missing optional
              position helpers, and       detail may reduce header or
              legacy face, prompt, or     navigation richness but must
              ID inference                not create a permission action
                                          or completed response.

   The lifecycle subscription currently consumes ‘init-started’,
‘init-finished’, ‘session-selected’, ‘session-restored’,
‘input-submitted’, ‘turn-complete’, and ‘error’.  Dedicated
subscriptions consume ‘permission-request’, ‘permission-response’, and
‘tool-call-update’.  Add new semantic behavior at the public event layer
when Agent Shell exposes it; keep renderer and private fallbacks
isolated and covered by compatibility tests.

16.3.6 WSL2 Omnivox development staging
---------------------------------------

The ordinary WSL2 user route is implemented by
‘bin/emacsvox-wsl-install’ and pinned by ‘etc/wsl-install.conf’.  Treat
either file as release tooling.  When advancing GNU Emacs or Omnivox,
update the version, archive name, URL, and SHA-256 together; exercise
x64 and ARM64 selection in the isolated installer tests; and perform one
real installation of the newly pinned archive before updating the user
manual.  The installer must remain per-user, must not invoke ‘sudo’ or
edit shell startup files, and must preserve an explicit ‘EMACS’ or
‘local.mk’ selection rather than silently replacing it.

   The installed Omnivox executable is recorded as an absolute WSL path
in the data-only ‘$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program’ file.
Launcher selection is explicit ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’, that per-user file,
developer staging, then ‘PATH’.  Keep the selection tests synchronized
with this order.  A configured but invalid path is a visible
configuration error; silently falling through would hide a stale or
damaged user installation.

   This section describes the reproducible source-build path, not the
ordinary pre-built WSL2 installation.  The complete source-build
prerequisite checklist is in *note Building Omnivox For WSL2::.
Dependency ownership is deliberately split across three boundaries:

Boundary      Supplied by            Dependencies
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Windows and   Maintainer             WSL interoperation,
WSL host                             ‘powershell.exe’, ‘wslpath’,
                                     Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or
                                     newer, Docker with Linux/AMD64
                                     support, Git, Make, download and
                                     checksum tools, standard GNU shell
                                     utilities, network access, and
                                     writable Windows ‘LocalAppData’.
Pinned        Emacsvox target        The immutable Rust/Debian
release                              container, MinGW cross tools and
toolchain                            runtime DLLs, CMake, Clang and
                                     libclang, eSpeak build
                                     dependencies, Roslyn, .NET 4.0
                                     reference assemblies, and official
                                     Windows Piper companion.  The exact
                                     versions, source commit, and
                                     downloaded-package hashes live in
                                     ‘servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock’.
Build         Git checkouts          The current Emacsvox checkout and
source                               the Omnivox checkout selected by
                                     ‘OMNIVOX_DIR’, including Omnivox's
                                     locked Rust dependencies and
                                     checksum-locked Flite and RuTTS
                                     source inputs.

   ‘make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain’ prepares the cached Roslyn
inputs, builds the Docker image, and validates this boundary before
compilation.  The full target then builds Omnivox's two 32-bit helper
sources deterministically, builds native Windows Omnivox plus its
RHVoice, Flite, and RuTTS helpers in the container, and stages their
licenses, provenance, and checksums.  The clean target also builds Piper
support and verifies that the pinned companion and main executable
identify the same Omnivox source commit.  A host Rust installation,
MinGW installation, Visual Studio, .NET SDK, or system eSpeak
installation is not an input to this release path.  RHVoice's native
library, languages, and voices remain external user-supplied runtime
inputs.  Flite's CMU US SLT voice is compiled into its checksum-locked
helper.  RuTTS's built-in male and female Russian voices are compiled
from the checksum-locked 6.3.3 source; RuLex is not included.

   After staging, the static verifier rechecks manifests, provenance,
the Windows-local copy, and all configured external inputs.  The
separate live verifier executes that exact Windows-local runtime,
prewarms the bounded eSpeak voice cache tied to the verified
content-addressed data, discovers the compiled Flite and RuTTS voices,
and requires real WAV synthesis from both helpers.  Use ‘make
verify-windows-omnivox-live’ to repeat only the executable smoke gate.

   ‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’ is an optional staging input, not a release
payload.  When supplied, the target validates the adjacent
configuration, copies both files into a content-addressed Windows-local
model cache, records their hashes, and writes the Windows path used by
the launcher.  The recursive runtime manifest deliberately excludes
model files.  A different model therefore creates a distinct local build
identity without changing the pinned companion.  The public-domain
engineering voice used in Omnivox CI is approved only for that bounded
test purpose and is not a model-distribution default.

   Maintainers testing tracked Emacsvox or Omnivox changes may use ‘make
windows-omnivox-dev’.  That target records both worktree-diff hashes and
labels the result as a development build.  It builds the RHVoice, Flite,
and RuTTS companions but omits Piper, because the currently pinned Piper
companion must not be mixed with later Omnivox source.  It also rejects
‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’.  Advance the checked Piper companion and source
commit together before these integrations enter the clean release path.
See ‘servers/omnivox-release/README.org’ for the pinned toolchain,
licensing, provenance, and restaging contract.

   To test Piper before that release-pin advance, commit and push a
clean Omnivox revision and run its ‘Piper Native Validation’ workflow.
Download the ‘omnivox-piper-windows-x64’ artifact, which contains the
companion archive and ‘piper-sha256sums.txt’, then stage it with a
reviewed model:

     OMNIVOX_PIPER_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHIVE=/absolute/path/to/omnivox-1.7.0-piper-windows-x64.zip \
       OMNIVOX_PIPER_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHIVE_SHA256=64-character-sha256 \
       OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx \
       make windows-omnivox-piper-dev

   This is a development-only bridge, not a release-pin bypass.  It
verifies the outer archive hash, rejects unsafe archive members and
symbolic links, checks the companion's internal manifest, and requires
its source provenance to name the exact clean Omnivox ‘HEAD’ and the
Windows MSVC target.  The staged runtime records the artifact hash and
companion tree hash and labels the companion
‘github-actions-native-development-build’ with version ‘development-ci’.
The version-looking archive filename comes from Omnivox's current
package metadata and does not assert that post-tag code was released.
The final live gate loads the separately cached model and requires Piper
to enumerate that voice and synthesize a non-empty WAV.

   The ordinary ‘windows-omnivox-dev’ target remains appropriate for
uncommitted Omnivox work because it rebuilds all included companions
locally and omits Piper.  The CI-artifact target deliberately rejects a
dirty Omnivox checkout: commit and push the change, let the native
workflow build that exact commit, then use its new artifact.  Emacsvox
itself may contain tracked changes; their diff hash remains part of the
local runtime identity.

   After one complete development runtime has been staged, use ‘make
windows-omnivox-main-dev’ for changes confined to the main Rust server
or its main-only audio output.  This guarded path uses the same pinned
toolchain to rebuild only ‘omnivox.exe’, derives a new content-addressed
runtime from the current hash-verified development payload, records the
original complete build and reused manifest in provenance, hard-links
the unchanged payload, and runs a focused live check of the new Windows
main executable.  It refuses reuse when helper, helper-protocol,
dependency, toolchain, or companion inputs changed; in that case rerun
‘make windows-omnivox-dev’.  Run ‘make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime
verify-windows-omnivox-live’ when a complete development audit is
useful.  The clean ‘make windows-omnivox’ release path always rebuilds
and verifies every payload and never uses incremental reuse.

   The development target accepts ‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY’ and
‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA’ together, plus an optional
‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG’ directory.  It resolves WSL or Windows absolute
paths, requires the C API DLL and the language/voice data layout,
records content digests in provenance and machine-local Windows path
pointers beside the selected runtime, and leaves all RHVoice files
external.  Those pointers let the launcher restore the variables for
every fresh Emacsvox process.  The runtime verifier rejects missing or
changed external inputs.  Omitting all three variables leaves RHVoice
unconfigured without affecting Flite, RuTTS, or the other engines.
RuTTS requires no external installation; the staged helper always
provides its built-in male and female Russian voices without RuLex.

   Do not bypass the Omnivox release target manually or clean either
repository to make its release guard pass.  Use ‘make windows-omnivox’
only for the reproducible clean-release path documented in the
user-facing WSL2 route.


